# 1Password env-file template — `op run --env-file=.env.op -- terraform <cmd>`
# resolves the op:// references to real secret values and injects them as env
# vars, so they never touch shell scrollback, history, or committed files.
#
# This file is committed (it holds op:// REFERENCES, not secret values). Anyone
# with read access to the referenced 1Password items gets the values; anyone
# without gets `op` errors and nothing leaks. Mirrors the AgenticOS
# state-backend/.env.op pattern.
#
# CF token: the storefront creds already exist in `Grove Infra` (Admin vault) —
# no minting needed. Ruleset read/import/plan requires `account_cloudflare_api_token`
# (verified live to read all 4 zones' rulesets); `cloudflare_zone_edit_token`
# returns auth-error 10000 on the rulesets API (no WAF read) so it is NOT used.
# Confirm the account token also has WAF/Cache *write* before the apply step.
#
# State backend creds: the Grove `grove-tf-state` Spaces bucket key, surfaced as
# AWS_* the S3 backend consumes (same convention as agenticos-tfstate). If the
# grove-tf-state bucket/key doesn't exist yet, bootstrap it like AgenticOS
# state-backend/ and store the key under these refs.

TF_VAR_cloudflare_api_token=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/account_cloudflare_api_token

# Optional: shared-secret header value that allowlists monitoring/health checks.
# Leave the item field empty (or omit this line) to disable the monitoring
# allowlist clause; the module drops it from the expression when unset.
TF_VAR_monitoring_secret_header_value=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/cloudflare_monitoring_shared_secret

# Remote state (grove-tf-state bucket-scoped Spaces key):
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/grove_tfstate_access_key_id
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/grove_tfstate_secret_key
